messages:
Maria: Quick question re: staging deploy — vectorizer pods failing with KMS decrypt AccessDenied right after a rollout. Looks like SA annotation goes in before the KMS grant/key policy is fully applied. What ordering do folks prefer in TF to avoid this race?

I have something like:
```hcl
resource "aws_iam_role" "irsa" { ... }
resource "kubernetes_service_account" "vec_sa" {
  metadata {
    annotations = {"eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn" = aws_iam_role.irsa.arn}
  }
}
resource "aws_kms_grant" "vec_grant" {
  key_id = aws_kms_key.vec_key.key_id
  grantee_principal = aws_iam_role.irsa.arn
  operations = ["Decrypt"]
}
```

But pods start and then fail decrypting for a few seconds. Any recommended pattern?

Jon: +1 saw this before. Two approaches that worked for us:
1) Prefer key policy permission (or a principal-based policy) so you don't rely on grants which can be eventually consistent.
2) If you must use grants, force Terraform ordering so grant exists before SA annotation that triggers pod rollout. e.g. add `depends_on = [aws_kms_grant.vec_grant]` on `kubernetes_deployment` (or on the `kubernetes_service_account` resource) so k8s objects wait.

Also note: KMS grant propagation sometimes lags the console/API for a few secs — add a tiny retry/wait step in your CI apply.

Priya: Another pattern: create the SA without the annotation first, apply the AWS pieces (role + grant), then patch the SA with the annotation as a separate apply step. Sequence: terraform apply infra (IAM+KMS) -> terraform apply k8s (with annotation). Keeps rollout explicit.

Maria: That sounds reasonable. Any lightweight way to automagically wait in TF instead of splitting applies? I'm trying to keep staging deploys single pipeline.

Sara: You can add a null_resource that uses local-exec to poll decrypt or describe-key until it succeeds, and then have the SA/deployment depend on it. Ugly but effective for single-pipeline flows. Example:
```hcl
resource "null_resource" "wait_kms" {
  triggers = { key = aws_kms_key.vec_key.key_id }
  provisioner "local-exec" {
    command = "./scripts/wait-for-kms-grant.sh ${aws_kms_key.vec_key.key_id} ${aws_iam_role.irsa.arn} 60"
  }
}
# then on your kubernetes resources: depends_on = [null_resource.wait_kms]
```

infra-bot: FYI — we have an automation check to catch SA annotation -> secret access races in staging: https://ci.example.com/pipelines/infra/checks/789 (will post logs if you hit the race) :robot_face:

Jon: Final note — if you control the key, put explicit allow for the IAM role in the key policy in addition to grants. Grants are nice for cross-account but key policy + IAM is simpler and more deterministic for same-account IRSA.

Maria: Thanks all — I'll try: 1) key-policy allow + 2) single-pipeline null_resource wait as a fallback. Will ping if I hit flakiness. :thumbsup:
